;; smtp.protocol as a Kotoba component -- the ESMTP capability surface and the
;; SASL payload framing, the last product semantics of RFC 5321 that lived only
;; in `.cljc`. Migrated as ONE component, not a slice (ADR-q9-whole-component-
;; build-migration, `:migration-unit :whole-component`).
;;
;; ## Already migrated, and deliberately NOT duplicated here
;;
;;   protocol_commands.kotoba  the command lines (EHLO / MAIL / RCPT / AUTH ...)
;;   protocol_response.kotoba  reply-line structure + RFC 3463 enhanced status
;;   protocol_core.kotoba      the SASL pick table over scalars
;;   message.kotoba            `dot-stuff` AND `mime-message`, taking
;;                             `msg :document`, both parity-tested
;;
;; A second `dot-stuff` and `mime-headers` were drafted here and deleted after
;; reading `message.kotoba`: two implementations of DATA transparency is how an
;; escaping rule drifts. Measured while checking: that one handles a 4096-byte
;; body, so there was nothing to improve on either.
;;
;; ## Host, as mechanism
;;
;;   base64        bytes and an alphabet, no SMTP meaning. The framing below
;;                 produces exactly the string the host base64s, which is where
;;                 the protocol decision lives.
;;   line joining  the socket yields lines; the host hands over the block this
;;                 file parses -- it already holds it as the reply text.
;;
;; ## The admitted text surface, measured 2026-09-09 by asking the compiler
;;
;; ADMITTED and lowered to js: string-fold-case, string-replace-all,
;;   string-contains?, string-join, string-concat, string-substring,
;;   string-length, string=?, string-byte-length, string-code-point-at,
;;   string-from-i64
;; ADMITTED BY `-M check` BUT NO js LOWERING (`unsupported KIR operation`, so
;;   `-M test` dies on the js target while `check` answers `:ok true`):
;;   string-upper, string-split-count
;; REFUSED (`:kotoba.error/subset-reject`): string-index-of, string-split-at,
;;   string-head, string-starts-with?, string-trim, string-value, min
;;
;; Case folding therefore uses `string-fold-case`, which is the right operation
;; for a case-INSENSITIVE comparison anyway, and counting is explicit.
;;
;; ## Why the code has this shape: keep the scan inside a builtin
;;
;; The emitted artifact charges ONE FUEL UNIT PER FUNCTION ENTRY against a
;; hard-coded 512 per module instance (`let fuel=512`; amu's
;; `fuel_estimate.cljc` calls it a function-entry charge model). There is no
;; flag for it.
;;
;; An earlier draft of this file hand-rolled case folding as a 26-branch table
;; and re-scanned the block from the start for every line. It answered
;; correctly on a 40-character fixture and threw `fuel-exhausted` at 50
;; characters of EHLO block -- and was about to be recorded as `:blocked` on the
;; language. It was not the language: `string-upper` was assumed absent after
;; grepping ONE backend file, and the O(n^2) shape was mine.
;;
;; So every predicate below is a `string-contains?` over a normalised block --
;; one entry, any length. Only `extension-value` walks characters, once.
;; Measured after the rewrite: one instance affords 9 `extension-value`, 11
;; `has-extension?` or 3 `auth-code` calls over a real 175-character response.
;;
;; ## Semantics preserved from the oracle
;;
;;   * the greeting (line 0) carries no extension and is dropped
;;   * RFC 1869 4.5: keywords are case-insensitive
;;   * an ADVERTISED extension with no value (STARTTLS) differs from an ABSENT
;;     one -- `extension-value` cannot say which, so `has-extension?` is a
;;     separate export rather than a length test
;;   * a mechanism matches only as a WHOLE token: XAUTH must not answer for
;;     AUTH, CLIENTTOKEN must not answer for PLAIN-CLIENTTOKEN
;;
;; A map cannot be returned, so the surface is positional over the block
;; instead of `{"AUTH" "PLAIN LOGIN"}` -- the design change protocol_response
;; already made for the reply line (ADR-2608650000 decision 6).
;;
;; ## Parity, and what it found
;;
;; `test/smtp/protocol_esmtp_kotoba_parity_test.cljk` is this repository's parity
;; standard applied here: it compiles both guests to KIR and executes every export
;; against the oracle `smtp.protocol` over nine EHLO blocks (real Gmail, minimal,
;; no AUTH, no SIZE, lower-case keywords, CRLF, bare keyword only, greeting only,
;; SIZE with no value), asserts the `.cljk` twin agrees with this file, and carries
;; a mutation control -- make `after-first-line` a no-op and the guest claims the
;; server greeting is an extension, which the control checks CHANGED the emitted
;; value before checking it disagrees with the oracle.
;;
;; It found two things that both harnesses had passed:
;;
;;   1. `extension-value` folded the VALUE as well as the keyword, answering
;;      `login plain xoauth2` where the oracle answers `LOGIN PLAIN XOAUTH2`. RFC
;;      1869 4.5 makes keywords case-insensitive and nothing makes values so.
;;      Fixed by matching in the folded block and reading from the raw one.
;;   2. `extension-count` counted 1 for a reply with NO extensions -- an empty
;;      section still sits between the two wrapping newlines -- and would also
;;      have counted a blank line and a trailing newline. It counts lines with
;;      content now.
;;
;; The twin keeps annotations on six exports and infers the other twelve. That
;; split is measured: strip more and unconstrained parameters fall back to `:i64`,
;; which surfaces as a type mismatch AT THE CALL SITE naming the caller rather
;; than the callee -- inference does not flow backwards from an annotated
;; parameter to its caller's. `creds-code` is in the annotated set for a sharper
;; reason: with its annotations off, inference typed its two BOOLEANS as i64, and
;; the only place that showed was the emitted artifact (`assertI64` where the
;; annotated build has `assertBool`). Both harnesses passed with that divergence
;; in place; comparing the two emitted files is what caught it. With those six
;; annotated, the two sources emit byte-identical js apart from the filename and
;; the two provenance digests.
;;
;; `.cljc` remains the oracle. `bin/protocol_behaviour.cljk` carries the
;; exhaustive value table (24 behaviours, real Gmail block, fresh instance per
;; call) and prints the fuel costs it measures.
;;
;; Compile / run (the CLI needs -M and an ABSOLUTE path):
;;   kotoba -M test /abs/kotoba/smtp/protocol.kotoba --json
;;   kbb --backend sci bin/protocol_behaviour.cljk

(ns smtp.protocol
  (:export [normalize normalize-raw extension-count extension-value has-extension?
            mechanism-count advertises-mechanism? max-size
            creds-code auth-code plain-credentials xoauth2-credentials
            test-normalize test-greeting-is-not-an-extension test-whole-token
            test-auth-code test-max-size test-sasl]))

;; ------------------------------------------------------------- the block
;;
;; One shape for every predicate: case-folded, CRLF folded to LF, THE GREETING
;; LINE REMOVED, and a newline at each end so every remaining line is
;; surrounded by them. That is what lets a keyword test be a substring test.
;;
;; Dropping the greeting is not tidiness. Measured 2026-09-09: with line 0 left
;; in, `has-extension?` answered TRUE for "smtp.gmail.com", because the greeting
;; begins its own line and NEWLINE + domain + SPACE is exactly the needle an
;; extension with a value matches. An earlier draft argued in a comment that the
;; greeting could not match; the comment was wrong and the test that asserts the
;; greeting is not an extension is what said so.

(defn- after-first-line [s :string i :i64] :string
  (if (>= i (string-length s))
    ""
    (if (string=? (string-substring s i (+ i 1)) "\n")
      (string-substring s (+ i 1) (string-length s))
      (after-first-line s (+ i 1)))))

(defn- wrapped [block :string] :string
  (string-concat
    (string-concat "\n" (after-first-line (string-replace-all block "\r\n" "\n") 0))
    "\n"))

;; Two normalisations of the same block, at the same offsets: `normalize` is
;; case-folded and every KEYWORD test runs against it, `normalize-raw` keeps the
;; case and every VALUE is extracted from it.
;;
;; Why both. RFC 1869 4.5 makes keywords case-insensitive, and nothing makes an
;; extension's VALUE case-insensitive -- the oracle upper-cases the keyword and
;; returns the value untouched. Folding the whole block made the guest answer
;; `login plain xoauth2` where the oracle answers `LOGIN PLAIN XOAUTH2`.
;; Mechanism matching folds both sides so it did not notice, and the parity test
;; against the oracle is what found it.
;;
;; The two agree on offsets because ASCII case folding is length-preserving. It
;; is NOT length-preserving in general (U+1E9B folds to two code points), so a
;; keyword outside ASCII could shift them; RFC 5321 4.1.2 keeps EHLO keywords in
;; ASCII, and this is a stated boundary rather than a silent assumption.
(defn normalize [block :string] :string
  (string-fold-case (wrapped block)))

(defn normalize-raw [block :string] :string
  (wrapped block))

(defn- blankish? [c :string] :bool
  (or (string=? c " ") (string=? c "\t")))

;; Extension lines with content, over the section AFTER the greeting. Counting
;; newlines instead was wrong three ways and the oracle caught one of them: a
;; block with no extensions at all has an empty section, which still sits between
;; the two wrapping newlines and counted as 1. A trailing newline and a blank line
;; are the other two -- `parse-extensions` skips blank lines and `split-lines`
;; drops the trailing empty, so neither is an extension.
(defn- count-lines [s :string i :i64 acc :i64 seen? :bool] :i64
  (if (>= i (string-length s))
    (if seen? (+ acc 1) acc)
    (if (string=? (string-substring s i (+ i 1)) "\n")
      (count-lines s (+ i 1) (if seen? (+ acc 1) acc) false)
      (count-lines s (+ i 1) acc
                   (or seen? (not (blankish? (string-substring s i (+ i 1)))))))))

(defn extension-count [block :string] :i64
  (count-lines (after-first-line (string-replace-all block "\r\n" "\n") 0) 0 0 false))

;; An extension is advertised when its keyword occupies a whole line, followed
;; either by a space (it has a value) or by the newline (it does not). The
;; leading newline in the needle is what stops SIZE matching inside XSIZE.
(defn- advertised-with-value? [nb :string name :string] :bool
  (string-contains? nb (string-concat (string-concat "\n" name) " ")))

(defn- advertised-bare? [nb :string name :string] :bool
  (string-contains? nb (string-concat (string-concat "\n" name) "\n")))

(defn has-extension? [block :string name :string] :bool
  (let [nb (normalize block)
        folded (string-fold-case name)]
    (or (advertised-with-value? nb folded) (advertised-bare? nb folded))))

;; ------------------------------------------------- the one scan in the file
;;
;; The value of a keyword is the rest of its line. There is no
;; `string-index-of` and no `string-split-at`, so this is the one place
;; characters are walked -- once, forward. `min` has no admitted lowering
;; either, so the window is guarded by `and` rather than clamped: measured,
;; `and` short-circuits on jvm-kir, js and wasm, which is what makes the guard
;; sufficient rather than decorative.

(defn- keyword-here? [nb :string name :string i :i64] :bool
  (and (<= (+ i (+ (string-length name) 2)) (string-length nb))
       (and (string=? (string-substring nb i (+ i 1)) "\n")
            (and (string=? (string-substring nb (+ i 1)
                                             (+ i (+ (string-length name) 1)))
                           name)
                 (string=? (string-substring nb (+ i (+ (string-length name) 1))
                                             (+ i (+ (string-length name) 2)))
                           " ")))))

(defn- line-tail [nb :string name :string i :i64] :i64
  (if (>= i (string-length nb))
    -1
    (if (keyword-here? nb name i)
      (+ i (+ (string-length name) 2))
      (line-tail nb name (+ i 1)))))

(defn- value-from [nb :string i :i64 acc :string] :string
  (if (>= i (string-length nb))
    acc
    (if (string=? (string-substring nb i (+ i 1)) "\n")
      acc
      (value-from nb (+ i 1) (string-concat acc (string-substring nb i (+ i 1)))))))

(defn extension-value [block :string name :string] :string
  ;; `wrapped` once, then fold. Calling `normalize` and `normalize-raw` would walk
  ;; the block twice -- `after-first-line` is the only per-character step in
  ;; either, and `string-fold-case` is a builtin that costs one unit at any
  ;; length. Measured: two walks doubled this export from 57 to 102 fuel units and
  ;; pushed the whole suite back over the 512 an instance has.
  (let [raw (wrapped block)
        nb (string-fold-case raw)
        folded (string-fold-case name)]
    (if (advertised-with-value? nb folded)
      (let [from (line-tail nb folded 0)]
        ;; found in the folded block, READ from the raw one: the keyword match is
        ;; case-insensitive and the value it points at is not.
        (if (< from 0) "" (value-from raw from "")))
      "")))

;; ------------------------------------------------------------- SASL (AUTH)
;;
;; A mechanism matches only as a WHOLE token: the needle is surrounded by the
;; separators a token can have, which keeps PLAIN from answering for
;; PLAIN-CLIENTTOKEN.

(defn- token? [v :string name :string] :bool
  ;; BOTH sides fold. `v` now arrives with its case intact (`extension-value`
  ;; reads values out of the unfolded block), and folding only the needle made
  ;; every mechanism test answer false against a server that advertises in upper
  ;; case -- which is every server.
  (and (> (string-length v) 0)
       (string-contains? (string-concat (string-concat " " (string-fold-case v)) " ")
                         (string-concat (string-concat " " (string-fold-case name)) " "))))

(defn- tokens [s :string i :i64 acc :i64 in? :bool] :i64
  (if (>= i (string-length s))
    acc
    (if (string=? (string-substring s i (+ i 1)) " ")
      (tokens s (+ i 1) acc false)
      (tokens s (+ i 1) (if in? acc (+ acc 1)) true))))

(defn mechanism-count [block :string] :i64
  (tokens (extension-value block "AUTH") 0 0 false))

(defn advertises-mechanism? [block :string name :string] :bool
  (token? (extension-value block "AUTH") name))

;; 0 neither, 1 token, 2 password, 3 both -- the packing `protocol_core` takes,
;; because `max-parameters` is 5 and the pick already spends four.
(defn creds-code [has-token? :bool has-password? :bool] :i64
  (if has-token?
    (if has-password? 3 1)
    (if has-password? 2 0)))

;; 0 none, 1 XOAUTH2, 2 PLAIN, 3 LOGIN, 4 unsupported. `auth-pick` over the
;; block the host already holds, which is what the oracle's `auth-pick-from`
;; does over a set.
;;
;; The AUTH value is read ONCE and threaded through. Measured: calling
;; `advertises-mechanism?` three times plus `mechanism-count` re-scanned the
;; block four times and cost more than an instance has, so `auth-code` threw
;; `fuel-exhausted` on a real EHLO block while every one of its parts answered
;; correctly alone. The budget is charged per function entry: the fix is fewer
;; passes, not smaller inputs.
(defn auth-code [block :string creds :i64] :i64
  (let [v (extension-value block "AUTH")]
    (cond
      (and (token? v "XOAUTH2") (or (= creds 1) (= creds 3))) 1
      (and (token? v "PLAIN") (or (= creds 2) (= creds 3))) 2
      (and (token? v "LOGIN") (or (= creds 2) (= creds 3))) 3
      (> (tokens v 0 0 false) 0) 4
      :else 0)))

;; RFC 4616: NUL authzid NUL authcid NUL passwd, the authorization identity
;; empty because a client authenticating as itself does not assume another.
;; The host base64s this.
(defn plain-credentials [user :string pass :string] :string
  (string-concat (string-concat "\0" user)
                 (string-concat "\0" pass)))

;; Google's XOAUTH2, which Microsoft also accepts. The NUL is its own literal:
;; measured, a NUL escape IMMEDIATELY FOLLOWED BY A LETTER is refused by the
;; reader on the compile path (`:kotoba/source-read-failed`) while a lone one,
;; two in a row, and one after a letter all read. `-M check` accepts the file
;; either way, which is its own finding. Concatenating gives the same bytes.
(defn xoauth2-credentials [user :string token :string] :string
  (string-concat
    (string-concat (string-concat "user=" user)
                   (string-concat "\0" "auth=Bearer "))
    (string-concat token "\0\0")))

;; ------------------------------------------------- SIZE (RFC 1870) digits

(defn- digit [s :string i :i64] :i64
  (let [c (string-substring s i (+ i 1))]
    (cond
      (string=? c "0") 0 (string=? c "1") 1 (string=? c "2") 2
      (string=? c "3") 3 (string=? c "4") 4 (string=? c "5") 5
      (string=? c "6") 6 (string=? c "7") 7 (string=? c "8") 8
      (string=? c "9") 9 :else -1)))

(defn- digits [s :string i :i64 acc :i64] :i64
  (if (>= i (string-length s))
    acc
    (if (< (digit s i) 0)
      acc
      (digits s (+ i 1) (+ (* acc 10) (digit s i))))))

;; -1 for absent or unparseable, which the oracle spells `nil`. A server MAY
;; advertise SIZE with no value (RFC 1870 6.1, no fixed maximum) and that is not
;; a limit of zero -- `has-extension?` answers whether it was advertised, this
;; answers what the limit IS.
(defn max-size [block :string] :i64
  (let [v (extension-value block "SIZE")]
    (if (= (string-length v) 0)
      -1
      (if (< (digit v 0) 0) -1 (digits v 0 0)))))

;; --------------------------------------------------------------- the tests
;;
;; `test-*` returns i64 1 on pass (the T9.3 harness contract), and these run on
;; jvm-kir, js AND wasm -- the only evidence the component works on the runtime
;; it ships to.
;;
;; The budget is the SUITE'S: the harness runs every test in ONE module instance
;; with 512 units. Measured per test against this implementation -- normalize 14,
;; greeting 128, whole-token 171, auth-code 73, max-size 57, sasl 3 -- about 446,
;; so there is roughly 66 units of headroom and a seventh test of any weight will
;; not fit. A suite of nine covering the same ground came to 786 and failed as
;; `fuel-exhausted` from the sixth test onward, with every one of those tests
;; returning 1 when run alone.
;;
;; So the exhaustive table (23 behaviours, real Gmail block, fresh instance per
;; call) is `bin/protocol_behaviour.cljk`, and that script PRINTS the costs it
;; measures -- including these -- so these numbers cannot quietly go stale.
;;
;; The two assertions that need a REAL response keep it: case folding across a
;; whole EHLO reply, and the greeting. The rest use a small block, where the
;; behaviour under test does not depend on size.

(defn- small [] :string
  "g\nSTARTTLS\nSIZE 10\nAUTH PLAIN PLAIN-CLIENTTOKEN")

(defn- gmail [] :string
  (string-concat
    (string-concat
      (string-concat "smtp.gmail.com at your service, [1.2.3.4]\n" "SIZE 35882577\n")
      (string-concat "8BITMIME\n"
                     "AUTH LOGIN PLAIN XOAUTH2 PLAIN-CLIENTTOKEN OAUTHBEARER XOAUTH\n"))
    (string-concat "ENHANCEDSTATUSCODES\nPIPELINING\n" "CHUNKING\nSMTPUTF8")))

;; The greeting is dropped, the rest is case-folded, and no CR survives. All
;; three are load-bearing for the substring tests above.
(defn test-normalize [] :i64
  (if (and (string=? (normalize "GREETING\r\nSize 10\r\nStartTLS")
                     "\nsize 10\nstarttls\n")
           (not (string-contains? (normalize "a\r\nb") "\r")))
    1 0))

;; On the REAL response: the greeting is line 0 and is not an extension, and
;; this is the assertion that caught it answering TRUE for the server domain.
(defn test-greeting-is-not-an-extension [] :i64
  (if (and (not (has-extension? (gmail) "smtp.gmail.com"))
           (string=? (extension-value (gmail) "size") "35882577"))
    1 0))

;; CLIENTTOKEN is a substring of PLAIN-CLIENTTOKEN and was never offered on its
;; own; a client that tries it authenticates with a mechanism the server does
;; not have. The same pair runs against Gmail's six mechanisms in the script.
(defn test-whole-token [] :i64
  (if (and (advertises-mechanism? (small) "plain-clienttoken")
           (not (advertises-mechanism? (small) "clienttoken")))
    1 0))

;; The pick table. The four real-block cases -- token / password / neither /
;; server-named-none, the last distinguishing "no mechanism we support" from
;; "no mechanism at all" -- are in the script.
(defn test-auth-code [] :i64
  (if (= (auth-code (small) 2) 2) 1 0))

;; The digit scan and the -1 for absent. RFC 1870's number does not get harder
;; with more extensions around it.
(defn test-max-size [] :i64
  (if (and (= (max-size (small)) 10)
           (= (max-size "greeting\nSTARTTLS") -1))
    1 0))

(defn test-sasl [] :i64
  ;; 1 + 4 + 1 + 6 = 12, and 6 + 1 + 13 + 1 + 1 = 22, only if each NUL is
  ;; exactly one character.
  (if (and (= (string-length (plain-credentials "user" "secret")) 12)
           (= (string-length (xoauth2-credentials "u" "t")) 22))
    1 0))
